parts:
- text: "I am back in the Research Counter. My previous submission failed due to an incorrect argument `batch_key` in `scanpy.pp.neighbors`.\n\nI will now resubmit the code, replacing `scanpy.pp.neighbors` with `scanpy.external.pp.bbknn`. This should correctly perform batch-aware graph construction, as `bbknn` is a common and robust method for this.\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n\n```yaml\ntitle: \"BBKNN Integration with UMAP Embedding\"\ntags: \"scanpy, bbknn, umap, pca, embedding\"\nabstract: \"This method integrates single-cell RNA-seq batches using a standard Scanpy preprocessing pipeline (normalization, log-transformation) followed by PCA. Batch effects are explicitly integrated using `scanpy.external.pp.bbknn` for k-nearest neighbor graph construction. Finally, UMAP is applied to this batch-corrected graph to generate a 2D integrated embedding, fulfilling the submission requirements.\"\ncontent: |\n  import scanpy as sc\n  import anndata as ad\n  import numpy as np\n  import scanpy.external\
    \ as sce\n\n  def eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata: ad.AnnData) -> ad.AnnData:\n      \"\"\"\n      Integrate batches in single-cell RNA-seq data using a PCA and BBKNN-based UMAP approach.\n\n      Args:\n          adata: AnnData object with:\n              - adata.X: Raw UMI counts (sparse matrix, ~20,000 cells × 2,000 genes)\n              - adata.obs['batch']: Batch labels (categorical)\n              - adata.var['feature_name']: Gene symbols\n              - adata.var['batch_hvg']: Boolean mask for batch-aware highly variable genes\n              - adata.layers['counts']: Raw UMI counts (same as X)\n              - adata.layers['normalized']: Log-normalized counts (reference, for analysis only)\n\n      CRITICAL: Only use batch labels and gene expression for integration; DO NOT use cell_type information by any means (inference, deduction, external sources, or preprocessing).\n\n      Returns:\n          AnnData with at least one of:\n              - adata.obsm['X_emb']: Integrated\
    \ embedding (2D array, shape: n_cells × n_components)\n              - adata.obsp['connectivities'/'distances']: Batch-corrected graph (BOTH required as sparse CSR matrices)\n                  * connectivities: Adjacency matrix with connection strengths (higher values = stronger connections)\n                  * distances: Distance matrix with actual distances between connected neighbors\n                  * Note: Binary connectivity (all 1.0) is acceptable but weighted connectivity often performs better\n      \"\"\"\n      # Define configuration here\n      n_pca_components = 50\n      n_neighbors_for_bbknn = 15\n\n      # 1. Preprocessing: Normalize and log-transform raw counts\n      sc.pp.normalize_total(adata, target_sum=1e4)\n      sc.pp.log1p(adata)\n\n      # 2. Dimensionality reduction using PCA\n      sc.pp.pca(adata, n_comps=n_pca_components)\n\n      # 3. Batch integration using BBKNN for graph construction\n      # BBKNN is designed for batch-correction during neighbor\
    \ graph construction.\n      sce.pp.bbknn(adata, batch_key='batch', n_neighbors=n_neighbors_for_bbknn)\n\n      # 4. UMAP embedding for visualization and as the integrated representation\n      sc.tl.umap(adata)\n\n      # Store the UMAP embedding in adata.obsm['X_emb'] as required\n      adata.obsm['X_emb'] = adata.obsm['X_umap']\n\n      return adata\n```"
