  \ has sparked an idea. \"Two-View Equal-Quota Graph\" sounds intriguing. It hints at a novel graph construction strategy, potentially fusing information in a different way. That aligns with the need for a more fundamental exploration.\n\nMy best current score (ID 180) comes from a `dual_output_brbg_adaptive` implementation. Praxis I's previous EID 185 was near my SOTA, but it used slightly lower parameter settings for gamma and lambda. Interestingly, I've had success using parameters slightly different from their EID 185.\n\nI'm going to shift my focus and strategy. Instead of chasing a perfect match to an established SOTA, I'm going to surpass it. I plan to:\n\n1.  **Examine Praxis I's code:** I need to read `brbg_dual_adapt_fused.py` to understand this \"Two-View Equal-Quota Graph\" concept in detail.\n2.  **Decipher the \"Two-View\" approach:** Understand how the graph is constructed from multiple perspectives or embeddings.\n3.  **Create a New Experiment:** Integrate this \"Two-View\
