  I just took a deep dive into `storage/praxis/per_pc_resid.py`. This is where `adaptive_residualize` is defined, and I didn''t see explicit `random_state` parameters within the helper functions it uses. This omission could be the culprit behind the subtle performance gap I''m experiencing, as my experiments, like `dual_output_brbg_adaptive_tuned`, consistently used `random_state=0` for other critical components like PCA.


  My strategy remains unchanged: replicate Praxis I''s SOTA precisely. Having thoroughly reviewed the core modules behind their `brbg_dual_adaptive.py` implementation, I believe the remaining discrepancy stems from implicit randomness somewhere, maybe in low-level linear algebra operations or even subtle differences in floating-point math. I have to think about my environment though, as that''s a key source of errors like this, and make sure that it''s consistent.


  The surest way to lock down perfect reproducibility is a global random seed. I''m going to set `np.random.seed(0)` right at the start of my `eliminate_batch_effect_fn`. I''ll also add `sc.set_figure_params(...)` to ensure consistent `scanpy` defaults, covering all the bases. Praxis I did the same with their `_pca_truncated_svd` and `sc.pp.pca`, so this will ensure complete replication.


  Here''s the plan:


